  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1118 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nThe forecast period begins with high pressure over the Gulf Coast \nstates and up into Arkansas. A upper ridge remains over the Great \nPlains and a shortwave trough is over the Central High Plains. A \ncold front continues to drop south through eastern Nebraska and \nwestern Iowa. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms have developed this evening across eastern \nColorado and western Kansas. Models have the storms currently in \nsouthwestern Kansas lifting northeast while the Colorado storms move \neast. After 2am, models have storms moving into northeastern Kansas \nand possibly into extreme southeastern Nebraska. There is still some \nuncertainty in how far north storms would potentially be able to get \nwith some models showing storms reaching as far north as Fremont and \nNorfolk, while others keep things dry north of I-80. The HRRR has \ndeviated its expected track of these storms a little further north \nthe past few runs, putting the northern edge closer to the I-80 \ncorridor which seems a reasonable solution. Precipitable water \nvalues approaching or exceeding 2.0\" will be possible. Some gusty \nwinds and heavy rainfall will be possible with these storms. \nIsolated hail cannot be entirely ruled out. There is a small chance \nfor some patchy fog developing overnight, particularly for areas \nnorth of the front; however northwesterly to north-northwesterly \nwinds may not be as conducive for it.\n\nShowers/storms will taper off west to east Sunday morning. Expect \nsome slightly cooler temperatures as the front continues to push \nthrough the region. Highs will range from the upper 70s to mid-80s. \nDrier conditions are expected heading into the afternoon and skies \nare expected to clear out. Some hazy skies might be visible as \nwildfire smoke from the PACNW and Canada filters in with north to \nnorth-northwest winds expected. \n\nMonday, a ridge sets up over the western CONUS with northwest flow \nover the Great Plains. Temperatures warm slightly, returning to the \nlow to mid-80s for most areas during the afternoon. An upper low \nwill be moving across Manitoba and Saskatchewan while a shortwave \ntrough is expected to move through the Great Plains. Showers and \nstorms, a few of which could become strong to severe are expected \nMonday afternoon/evening.\n\nTuesday brings some slightly cooler temperatures to northeastern \nNebraska/northwestern Iowa as a cold front slides in from the north. \nCurrently, the forecast has highs in the upper 70s to mid-80s for \nareas along and north of I-80. Areas toward the Kansas/Nebraska \nborder will be in the mid to upper 80s. Tuesday afternoon and \nevening will bring a chance for some strong to severe thunderstorms \nacross the region.\n\nWednesday through Friday, the ridge amplifies over the western CONUS \nand a series of shortwaves move across the Great Plains. There will \nbe a few hit or miss chances for a shower or storm through this \ntimeframe. Cooler temps (70s and 80s for highs) are expected through \nthe end of the week.",
